00:07:10.735 --> 00:07:12.110
Can you tell us
a little bit more

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:15.710
about how you got up to
date in running with Agones?

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:16.550
KAMI MAY: Sure.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:19.760
So when we planned this
game, we didn't really

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:23.840
have any experience on how
to solve the online problems,

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:26.790
because we have never made
a multiplayer game before.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:30.120
And so we looked
into a few options.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:31.670
We did a lot of research.

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:35.630
And we stuck that Kubernetes
must be the way to go,

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:39.530
because in terms of price
and resource management,

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:42.920
it would cost the least
to do what we need.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:46.460
And we looked into a
few options on where

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:50.270
to go with the Kubernetes,
and we found Agones project.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.730
At that time, it
was extremely early

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:54.420
in the development process.

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.030
It was in August 2018.

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:58.160
But we decided to
give it a go anyways,

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:00.050
because we have
such a small team.

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:01.730
It was only two
server developers

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:03.230
working on this game.

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:06.680
One of them was purely
a game server developer,

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:09.470
so he didn't do
any sort of DevOps.

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:12.780
And then there was me, who
had to figure it all out.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:16.820
And there was really no time
for me to write everything

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:18.950
and launch the game in a year.

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:21.140
So due to lack of
resources, we decided

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:23.030
to give a go to
Agones, even if it

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:25.550
was alpha project at that time.

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:27.840
We tried it and
it worked for us.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.280
It worked very well,
and we stuck with it.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:35.390
And it launched 0.1 just as we
launched our game, so that's

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:36.860
extremely good timing.

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:38.809
So we went into
production as Agones

00:08:38.809 --> 00:08:42.518
was released in full release
rather than any sort of beta.

00:08:42.518 --> 00:08:45.060
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Which is just
amazing timing, first of all.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:46.270
KAMI MAY: Yes, it was.

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:46.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.187
MARK MIRCHANDANI: But obviously,
if Mark Mandel were here,

00:08:49.187 --> 00:08:50.280
he could explain Agones.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.610
And I'm sure several of
our podcast listeners

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:53.720
have heard it before.

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:55.400
Let's give a basic
rundown for people

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:56.690
who may not have heard of it.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.540
What did Agones do that
was different from just

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:01.038
running Kubernetes by itself?

00:09:01.038 --> 00:09:01.580
KAMI MAY: OK.

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:06.560
So Agones is a custom
resource on top of Kubernetes,

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:09.890
and it has very basic
functionality that

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:12.930
is absolutely required
for game servers,

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:16.040
such as server
allocation and not

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:19.770
killing allocated servers that
already having players on it.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:23.150
So even if you roll out
the update in mid-session,

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:24.830
players that are
already in session

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:27.690
should never lose their
progress at that time.

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:30.860
So it had simple allocation.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:34.160
It had ability to scale,
even though it was not

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:37.220
at first in Agones when
we started using it,

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:40.040
and I had to write
custom code to do that.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:43.625
But in further releases,
they added those features.

00:09:43.625 --> 00:09:47.220
And there is also ability
to scale during live.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:51.410
So let's say we suddenly have
a massive surge in players.

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:55.140
We can simply change the buffer,
deploy, and with no downtime,

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:57.480
we have a lot more
available servers.

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:00.500
It also has a way
to add labels, so we

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:03.690
know what's going on in
that particular game mode.

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:06.170
It has different fleets, so
you can have different game

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:07.550
modes in different fleets.

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:10.490
So for example, our
game server code

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:13.370
is the same code for all modes.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:16.610
We just add different
variables to set environment

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:18.200
for different game mode.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.930
So all those features
were crucial for our game.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:25.388
And without Agones, I would
have to write it all myself

00:10:25.388 --> 00:10:26.180
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:28.692
So it sounds like there
were things that were hard,

00:10:28.692 --> 00:10:30.650
especially when you first
started using Agones,

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:32.330
because it was only 0.1.

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:35.167
So I can imagine that there
were some things that were hard,

00:10:35.167 --> 00:10:36.750
and some things that
were really easy.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:38.250
So can you tell us
a little bit more

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:40.830
about what you found
very hard dealing with,

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:42.910
and what you found
very, very simple?

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:45.640
KAMI MAY: So I guess
difficulties were the bugs.

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:46.740
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:49.560
So I guess when you
start using software,

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:51.640
you are aware that
it is alpha release.

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:54.900
But because you're so
brand-new to this whole scene,

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:57.000
you are unsure
whether it's a bug

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.010
or whether it works as expected.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:01.350
There were a lot of
conversations in Slack

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:03.960
where I would bring
it all up, and I was

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.000
able to find quite a few bugs.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.300
I think it was also because
we went into production

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:12.750
with Agones very early on
and we found a lot of things

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:15.060
that otherwise
simulated scenarios

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:16.710
wouldn't be able to catch.

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:20.700
There was a bug where,
if the pod shut down,

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:22.990
the game server would remain.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:25.600
And so the game server
would not point to any pod.

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:29.190
So those things were quite
difficult to deal with.

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:31.350
A lot of manual
work had to be done.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:35.190
And I guess the easy part
was literally the rest.

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:40.290
It was just very easy, even if
you have no previous experience

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:41.520
using Kubernetes.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:44.580
If you have some server
engineering knowledge,

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:45.955
it will be quite
straightforward.

00:11:45.955 --> 00:11:47.830
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah,
because you mentioned

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:51.000
that you were coming into this
with one other game server

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:51.697
engineer.

00:11:51.697 --> 00:11:53.280
And between the two
of you, you didn't

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.110
have a lot of
familiarity with-- sounds

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:56.670
like even Kubernetes itself.

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:59.670
KAMI MAY: No, we didn't use
Kubernetes at all before.

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:02.220
All we used was
Heroku, actually.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:05.400
Our previous games are
very single-player type

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:07.690
of games where you
have some validation

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:10.530
and it just needs to
have an HTTP server that

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:12.570
validates your actions.

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:16.440
In this case, we need a
TCP connection, validate

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:18.210
all the movements
of the players,

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:22.320
and this all has to be
separate servers for each game.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:27.250
So this was something that
we didn't really do before.

00:12:27.250 --> 00:12:29.640
We had some guidance
from Google once we

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:33.790
decided to go with Google,
and that was very helpful.

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:36.690
We had some courses given to us.

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:39.870
They gave us some credits to
view the Google University

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.792
type of thing, and that
was all very useful.

00:12:42.792 --> 00:12:43.875
JON FOUST: That's awesome.

00:12:43.875 --> 00:12:46.860
I can hear a lot of
troubleshooting coming

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:48.690
from Google, but I'm curious.

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:50.785
Working with Agones in
an open source manner--

00:12:50.785 --> 00:12:52.410
I know that you're
a part of the Slack,

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:53.993
because I'm also a
part of that Slack,

00:12:53.993 --> 00:12:56.610
and I know how quite frequently
you post in that Slack,

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:58.590
so I'm pretty sure you and Mark
are really good best friends.

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:00.132
But can you tell us
a little bit more

00:13:00.132 --> 00:13:04.590
about how it is dealing with a
gaming solution in open source?

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:07.180
KAMI MAY: I did have to
go through a lot of logs

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:10.810
in Agones sidecars to
find out what's going on--

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:14.580
when it is our fault, when it
is Agones doing something funny.

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:17.970
And I found that when
we got to the point

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:20.640
where we had to really
dig into the logs,

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:23.580
they were written so well
and so descriptively,

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:27.390
it would literally point to
the issue almost straightaway.

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:29.980
So that was extremely useful.

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:31.710
There's some times
that you're not

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:34.050
quite sure what you
can do about the issue,

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:37.170
and you go through
Agones' source code,

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:39.920
even though I've never
written a line in Go.

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:41.700
I just look through
the source code

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:44.410
to find where the
code is coming from,

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:46.410
where is this error
coming from, to try

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:48.490
and figure out what
is going wrong.

00:13:48.490 --> 00:13:51.750
And I think it being open
source really helped,

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:54.630
because I could really
find where the issue is

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:56.092
coming from sometimes.

00:13:56.092 --> 00:13:57.550
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So obviously, you

00:13:57.550 --> 00:14:00.090
were using Agones from when
it was still in testing phase.

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.670
And it sounds like there was
a lot of active communication

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:05.280
between you, Mark, and other
people working on it, which

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.613
is actually pretty cool, right?

00:14:07.613 --> 00:14:09.780
Sometimes you get to work
on an open source platform

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:11.700
and you can make
some pull requests

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:12.783
and those sorts of things.

00:14:12.783 --> 00:14:15.200
But it sounds like there was
a really active communication

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:15.720
channel.

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:19.290
How did that affect
your launch window?

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:21.570
When you've got a
deadline, game developers

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:23.190
often have a big
rush, especially

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:25.840
towards the end when they're
trying to get ready to launch.

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:27.690
How is that communication?

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:31.410
Did it help you, obviously,
in launching the game?

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:32.730
KAMI MAY: Yeah, I think so.

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:35.820
Actually, we had a lot of
calls ongoing in the year.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:40.260
We had meetings last year
with Google and Mark,

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:45.690
and we also had some third party
coming in, assessing our setup,

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:48.900
to tell us whether or not
we're production ready.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:50.880
They looked at our setup.

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:52.140
They said it was very good--

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:56.190
[LAUGHS] especially
considering the team size.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:58.050
There were some
really good pointers,

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:00.990
so there were difficult
questions we had to ask,

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:03.930
because we really didn't
know which direction to take.

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:07.950
And the example is a
global load balancer.

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:12.660
How do you load balance six
different regions under one IP?

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:15.160
Those things, we
were not aware of.

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:19.500
And asking Google really helped
us to come with solutions.

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:24.390
And we already had deadlines
set quite a long time ago

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:28.230
that was not
relevant to the help.

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:31.650
So we had a deadline that
was promised by our CEO

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:35.060
to Google and Apple,
and we had to deliver.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:38.460
And I guess there was
one hiccup that we had,

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:42.740
which was quite unpleasant,
is that our quota increase was

00:15:42.740 --> 00:15:43.670
really delayed.

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:46.490
And we were quite
unsure whether or not

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:49.070
we would get enough
quota in time for launch.

00:15:49.070 --> 00:15:52.190
However, once we emailed
our account manager,

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:53.780
that was all done so quickly.

00:15:53.780 --> 00:15:57.290
[LAUGHS] So I think it's
just reaching out to people

00:15:57.290 --> 00:15:59.120
at the right time is the key.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:00.818
And Google is extremely
helpful at that.

00:16:00.818 --> 00:16:01.610
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:04.050
So it sounds like
there were a couple

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:07.620
of things that seemed to start
to get to you towards launch.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:08.360
So I'm curious.

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:09.690
What happens post-launch?

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:13.100
What happens when your game's
running and everything looks

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:15.682
really well, but there
needs to be a plan?

00:16:15.682 --> 00:16:17.390
I know Agones scales
very well, but there

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:19.883
needs to be a plan for any
type of server improvements

00:16:19.883 --> 00:16:21.050
that can happen post-launch.

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:22.945
So can you tell us a
little bit about that?

00:16:22.945 --> 00:16:23.820
KAMI MAY: Yeah, sure.

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:28.280
So our further plan is
actually-- currently,

00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:32.210
we have nine clusters, so we're
live in nine different regions

00:16:32.210 --> 00:16:33.290
for now.

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:37.100
We'll Google assess in the
future and remove some of them

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:38.300
if they have no load.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:42.410
So currently, each region
is very independent,

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:44.300
and they all have
all the services

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:45.660
running in each region.

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:47.720
However, that's not
very cost-efficient,

00:16:47.720 --> 00:16:50.970
and some services are
not very time-sensitive.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:54.440
So we want to move all of
those into the central cluster,

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:59.640
probably in the US East region,
and save money on resources.

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:02.420
But also to improve updating.

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:05.329
So right now, deployments
are quite elaborate.

00:17:05.329 --> 00:17:07.550
We have to deploy
to each region.

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:11.660
And we would want to make
minimal changes to each region.

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:14.863
And having it all centralized
would make it a lot easier.

00:17:14.863 --> 00:17:16.280
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Well ultimately,

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:17.660
it's like a trade-off, right?

00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:20.060
It's a trade-off between
being able to have

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:21.710
these regions closer
to the people who

00:17:21.710 --> 00:17:24.980
are playing the game versus the
complexity of the deployment

00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:25.670
pipeline.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.310
But in the case of actually
playing a game, especially

00:17:28.310 --> 00:17:29.790
a networked game,
it's always going

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:32.117
to be to minimize the latency
for those players, right?

00:17:32.117 --> 00:17:32.700
KAMI MAY: Yes.

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:36.260
So game servers will most
definitely remain in all

00:17:36.260 --> 00:17:40.460
the regions that we want to
provide the non-sensitive data,

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:43.670
like just validating when
you change your name--

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:45.470
saving your preferences.

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:47.880
This doesn't require
immediate response,

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:50.507
so it's fine if it
has a bit of latency.

00:17:50.507 --> 00:17:51.590
JON FOUST: So I'm curious.

00:17:51.590 --> 00:17:53.720
How do you actually
measure exactly where

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:54.740
you want it to locate?

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:55.782
KAMI MAY: So this was a--

00:17:55.782 --> 00:17:57.490
[LAUGHS] big problem.

00:17:57.490 --> 00:17:59.430
We were looking
at the map and we

00:17:59.430 --> 00:18:02.870
were looking at what
zones does Google provide,

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:05.880
and really thinking
what we want to do.

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:09.950
So for launch, we really wanted
minimal latency for everyone

00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:10.830
around the world.

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:15.500
So we went above and beyond and
created nine different regions.

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:17.840
Once we have more
data, once we know

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:21.740
what ping is where, we
can assess and decide.

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:24.500
Maybe some regions are
not really required.

00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:27.860
Because in Asia, we have
three different regions.

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:29.730
That seems a bit of an overkill.

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:32.090
So for now, we have nine.

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.910
And we decided it just
by looking at the map

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:39.710
and thinking what latency would
be effective for everyone.

00:18:39.710 --> 00:18:44.570
Because this game can't hold
more than 150ms latency.

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:46.320
Post that, it gets hard to play.

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:48.320
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So that
means there's probably

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:51.740
a lot of work going into
the data collection of users

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:54.302
playing the game and
understanding what kind

00:18:54.302 --> 00:18:55.760
of metrics you can
measure on them.

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:58.250
Can you talk a little bit
more about getting information

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:00.482
from the players that
have played since launch?

00:19:00.482 --> 00:19:02.940
KAMI MAY: Well, all of this
data is coming from the client,

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:05.030
so we have a lot of
data analytics going

00:19:05.030 --> 00:19:07.145
into our BigQuery.

00:19:07.145 --> 00:19:10.520
I'm not a data analyst,
but our all data analytics

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:11.600
is also on Google.

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:15.470
And we have Looker dashboards
that we can check every day,

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:19.130
so that's one of the things
we usually do every morning.

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:22.130
We open the Looker here
and look what went wrong,

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:23.760
and hope nothing did.

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:26.870
So all of the analytics
comes from actual client.

00:19:26.870 --> 00:19:29.160
We sometimes get some
feedback from players,

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:32.300
but it's usually less
useful, because we

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:35.090
need to know exactly what
happened for that case.

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:36.640
JON FOUST: So I
could imagine that

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:38.720
dealing with a lot of
servers-- and there

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:41.220
has to be a lot of logs that
you have to deal with, as well.

00:19:41.220 --> 00:19:43.460
So I'm not sure if you're using
any Google products for that.

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:45.043
How do you trace the
logs for anything

00:19:45.043 --> 00:19:47.640
that can possibly go wrong
with any of your users?

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:50.540
KAMI MAY: Google has
amazing log tool.

00:19:50.540 --> 00:19:52.820
This was so good--

00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:53.900
life saving.

00:19:53.900 --> 00:19:56.810
It makes searching for
particular logs extremely easy.

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:58.820
Obviously we had to
put some work into it.

00:19:58.820 --> 00:20:02.390
We had to make sure that our
logs print out the data that

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:04.400
would be useful for debugging.

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:07.850
So we had many
iterations of releases

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:11.130
to know, oh, we are trying
to deal with this issue

00:20:11.130 --> 00:20:12.770
but we are missing this data.

00:20:12.770 --> 00:20:16.570
So we keep adding, to the point
where in production now, we

00:20:16.570 --> 00:20:19.560
are more or less confident
if something goes wrong,

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:22.460
we can find what happened
to you because we

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:24.020
have sufficient logging.

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:28.340
And Stackdriver makes
it so easy to find it.

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:29.780
It's so useful.

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:30.170
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Well, it sounds

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:31.712
like you've gotten
a lot of advantage

00:20:31.712 --> 00:20:34.915
out of using the Google Cloud
ecosystem as a whole, right?

00:20:34.915 --> 00:20:36.790
You mentioned BigQuery,
Stackdriver, and then

00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:38.440
of course, Agones on Kubernetes.

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:39.940
Are there any things
that you've run

00:20:39.940 --> 00:20:42.010
into that you feel
like we're missing,

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:44.093
or things that you feel
like would have helped you

00:20:44.093 --> 00:20:45.160
along that way faster?

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:47.950
KAMI MAY: I guess there are
some things that are there

00:20:47.950 --> 00:20:49.690
but are harder to set up.

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:53.950
So like, I'm really not a
professional here with VPCs

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:56.860
or load balancing stuff.

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:59.560
And I have to use
such as kubemci

00:20:59.560 --> 00:21:03.050
to set up global load
balancing across clusters

00:21:03.050 --> 00:21:08.020
and further dig into it
to add some HTTPS security

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:09.248
certificates on top of that.

00:21:09.248 --> 00:21:11.290
And all of these things
are quite difficult and--

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:13.420
