00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:31.238
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:35.560
What are the best
practices for setting up

00:29:35.560 --> 00:29:37.650
user accounts in Cloud IAM?

00:29:37.650 --> 00:29:40.330
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah, there is
definitely a tremendous amount

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:41.390
of best practices.

00:29:41.390 --> 00:29:43.810
But I think it's also
important for everyone,

00:29:43.810 --> 00:29:46.180
no matter what kind of role
you're using Google Cloud

00:29:46.180 --> 00:29:49.600
Platform in, to get at least a
baseline understanding of Cloud

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:50.470
IAM.

00:29:50.470 --> 00:29:54.160
So I'll link to a quick start
guide and to a checklist guide,

00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:57.220
but some of the bigger ones
that I've seen come up recently

00:29:57.220 --> 00:29:57.760
are--

00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:01.108
of course, Cloud recommends
this least-privilege principle.

00:30:01.108 --> 00:30:02.650
And this is the idea
that you're only

00:30:02.650 --> 00:30:04.990
giving people exactly the
permissions that they need,

00:30:04.990 --> 00:30:05.830
and no more.

00:30:05.830 --> 00:30:08.200
Typically, this is done
just to prevent mistakes

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:09.400
from happening, right?

00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:10.990
If you've got
someone who is going

00:30:10.990 --> 00:30:13.060
to be working in a
development environment,

00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:14.320
you want to give them
the right permissions

00:30:14.320 --> 00:30:15.490
for that development
environment.

00:30:15.490 --> 00:30:17.365
But you also want to
make sure that they only

00:30:17.365 --> 00:30:19.990
have permissions they need, such
as spinning up Compute Engine

00:30:19.990 --> 00:30:22.480
instances, rather than
spinning up some other products

00:30:22.480 --> 00:30:23.722
that you don't plan on using.

00:30:23.722 --> 00:30:25.180
That could be really
important when

00:30:25.180 --> 00:30:27.130
you're in a large
organization, because it'll

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:29.920
be really hard to track down if
someone accidentally spits up

00:30:29.920 --> 00:30:31.550
something very expensive.

00:30:31.550 --> 00:30:33.982
And then you don't realize
it until way down the road,

00:30:33.982 --> 00:30:35.440
and you've got this
big bill to pay

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:37.720
because of just a simple error.

00:30:37.720 --> 00:30:40.450
So it's always really important
to be very thoughtful about it,

00:30:40.450 --> 00:30:44.510
and that's why Cloud IAM also
lets you create custom roles.

00:30:44.510 --> 00:30:47.290
So I know we've all been in that
boat where we're just like, OK.

00:30:47.290 --> 00:30:48.790
Someone needs to
get something done.

00:30:48.790 --> 00:30:51.400
Let's just give them
project owner role,

00:30:51.400 --> 00:30:53.150
and then they'll
just take care of it.

00:30:53.150 --> 00:30:54.250
But that's really not--

00:30:54.250 --> 00:30:57.310
[LAUGHS] that's really not
the best way to do things,

00:30:57.310 --> 00:31:00.910
because you can easily get
yourself into trouble with just

00:31:00.910 --> 00:31:02.530
having someone, even if--

00:31:02.530 --> 00:31:04.510
it's usually just
by accident-- having

00:31:04.510 --> 00:31:07.960
some accidental resources
spin up or something shut off.

00:31:07.960 --> 00:31:10.640
So I recommend creating
custom roles for that.

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:13.540
And on top of that, you're
also able to assign roles

00:31:13.540 --> 00:31:15.640
to groups rather than users.

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:19.540
And this falls under this
idea of reasonable redundancy,

00:31:19.540 --> 00:31:23.290
where if you have someone who
is responsible for some very

00:31:23.290 --> 00:31:25.810
important thing on some
very important project,

00:31:25.810 --> 00:31:28.390
and then that person is no
longer around for some reason,

00:31:28.390 --> 00:31:31.060
or isn't available, you're
going to be in a lot of trouble

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:33.670
if that person has that
task they need to do.

00:31:33.670 --> 00:31:35.722
So a big example
for finance teams

00:31:35.722 --> 00:31:37.930
is making sure that you have
a billing administrator,

00:31:37.930 --> 00:31:39.763
because there are some
things only a billing

00:31:39.763 --> 00:31:40.640
administrator can do.

00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:43.000
So a great example for that
is, instead of assigning it

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:44.930
to a user, assign it to a group.

00:31:44.930 --> 00:31:46.730
And in G Suite, you
can have a group

00:31:46.730 --> 00:31:48.530
or you can have
groups in other ways.

00:31:48.530 --> 00:31:51.400
And once you have a group, you
can then add and remove people

00:31:51.400 --> 00:31:54.520
from that group as your staffs
or your employee might change,

00:31:54.520 --> 00:31:56.110
so that you're not
having to update

00:31:56.110 --> 00:31:57.910
that role every single time.

00:31:57.910 --> 00:32:00.560
You can just have this
trusted pool of users.

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:02.140
This can give you
an easy way to have

00:32:02.140 --> 00:32:04.360
that reasonable level of
redundancy in case someone

00:32:04.360 --> 00:32:07.330
isn't available, without
having to go in and edit IAM

00:32:07.330 --> 00:32:08.240
all the time.

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:10.770
So we'll link to those in
the checklist in the notes.

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:12.853
I think they're really
great for people to review,

00:32:12.853 --> 00:32:14.320
and I definitely
recommend people

00:32:14.320 --> 00:32:16.805
going to check out
the IAM interface

00:32:16.805 --> 00:32:18.430
and playing around
with it, just to get

00:32:18.430 --> 00:32:20.170
a quick feel of
how the roles work

00:32:20.170 --> 00:32:21.820
and maybe creating
a custom role.

00:32:21.820 --> 00:32:23.320
So that in the
future, when they get

00:32:23.320 --> 00:32:26.043
into larger and more
complex scenarios,

00:32:26.043 --> 00:32:27.460
you'll have a
better understanding

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:30.310
of what are the best
principles to enact there

00:32:30.310 --> 00:32:31.390
when you're adding them.

00:32:31.390 --> 00:32:32.182
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:32:32.182 --> 00:32:34.270
I've actually played
around with custom roles,

00:32:34.270 --> 00:32:36.640
and it's actually
what I use as a go-to

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:40.360
when I am asking someone to
work on something, whether it's

00:32:40.360 --> 00:32:43.953
internal or external,
and I want them to only

00:32:43.953 --> 00:32:46.120
be able to use, for example,
like a service account.

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:48.070
So I assign a role
to a service account

00:32:48.070 --> 00:32:52.930
and give them privilege and use
of that hard, actual service

00:32:52.930 --> 00:32:55.210
account-- the JSON file
you actually download.

00:32:55.210 --> 00:32:57.820
And they can use that
freely for whatever purposes

00:32:57.820 --> 00:32:58.820
that I give them.

00:32:58.820 --> 00:33:01.310
And it's really good, because
when I'm doing game demos,

00:33:01.310 --> 00:33:05.208
it's really good to allow
them to access and make

00:33:05.208 --> 00:33:07.000
any type of request
that you would normally

00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:09.768
do with an account
owner, or any small demo

00:33:09.768 --> 00:33:10.810
that you're working with.

00:33:10.810 --> 00:33:12.993
And only given them the
privileges that they need.

00:33:12.993 --> 00:33:15.160
That means that it's just
read and write privileges,

00:33:15.160 --> 00:33:16.202
and that's just about it.

00:33:16.202 --> 00:33:19.120
So custom roles has definitely
been a powerful feature

00:33:19.120 --> 00:33:21.250
that I've utilized,
and I definitely

00:33:21.250 --> 00:33:23.350
recommend our listeners
to take a good look at it.

00:33:23.350 --> 00:33:24.190
MARK MIRCHANDANI: We'll
make sure to include

00:33:24.190 --> 00:33:25.990
a link for service accounts
as well, because that's

00:33:25.990 --> 00:33:27.270
something I didn't mention.

00:33:27.270 --> 00:33:29.900
But that is another
kind of best practices.

00:33:29.900 --> 00:33:32.980
If you're talking about having
a human go in and do things,

00:33:32.980 --> 00:33:35.510
you're going to typically give
it to that user's account.

00:33:35.510 --> 00:33:37.600
But if you want to
have them call an API

00:33:37.600 --> 00:33:39.618
or work from a
machine or in code,

00:33:39.618 --> 00:33:41.410
you actually want to
use a service account,

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:44.737
because then you have even
more control over that access.

00:33:44.737 --> 00:33:46.570
So a couple of helpful
references for people

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:47.600
who are interested in that.

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:49.225
